<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  RM_Hediff_CaptivityMemory.xml                    FEVERWOOD_DIANOGA_PRISON_1

  Owner ruling 3, verbatim: "It remembers the tank... [a released one] should
  have large wildness, be very challenging to tame, and have a high chance of
  attacking if you fail." Ruled in SPIRIT, mechanism deliberately left open —
  "Whether that is a hediff, a per-pawn tame-chance penalty, a manhunter bias
  on failed taming, or a memory of the specific colony is open... Do not
  implement a generic wildness bump and call this delivered; the flavour he
  is naming is grudge, not difficulty."

  This is the DATA half of that call, not the numeric half: applied by
  RM_CompCapturedSpecimen.Escape() to every escapee, permanent, zero
  statOffsets/capMods. It exists so the animal genuinely carries "I was held"
  as a fact the game can query (Pawn.health.hediffSet.HasHediff), and so a
  later pass can hang the real grudge mechanism (a tame-chance penalty
  specifically against the colony that held it, a manhunter bias, whichever
  the follow-on item rules) off THIS marker rather than re-deriving "was this
  animal ever captive" from scratch. ⛔ Its own tame-chance/manhunter effect
  is UNSET — do not read this hediff's mere presence as "the grudge is
  built"; see FEVERWOOD_DIANOGA_TANK_TUNING_1.
-->
<Defs>

  <HediffDef>
    <defName>RM_CaptivityMemory</defName>
    <hediffClass>Hediff</hediffClass>
    <label>captivity memory</label>
    <description>This animal was once held in a prison tank and broke free. Whatever the encounter is meant to cost it in the wild — its willingness to ever trust a hand again — is not yet tuned; the fact of it is remembered here.</description>
    <defaultLabelColor>(0.55, 0.35, 0.6)</defaultLabelColor>
    <isBad>false</isBad>
    <tendable>false</tendable>
    <maxSeverity>1.0</maxSeverity>
    <initialSeverity>1.0</initialSeverity>
  </HediffDef>

</Defs>
